[{:seon.task/name "a-dependency-is-fetched-through-its-owner"
  :seon.task/plan "Done; what exists. Fetching a dependency is an outside-world effect, so it goes through the effect owner like file write-back and git (DECISIONS: Loading code is an effect; the PKG plan, the archived paper option3-PKG-plan.md (git history) in the tag `history/prototype`). The `:deps` kind in `resources/seon/system.edn` is performed by `seon.fx/resolve-deps`: `{:req {:deps <a deps.edn :deps map>}}` resolved by Jolt's own `jolt.deps/resolve-deps` (no resolver of ours: Jolt fetches `:mvn/version` and `:git/sha`, uses `:local/root`, caches in `.cpcache`), answering `{:value {:roots [absolute source roots]}}` or its one typed error `:seon.fx/unresolved` (in `resources/seon/schemas/seon.problems.edn`, docs/failure-points.md). Jolt answers a missing `:local/root` as a root with no error: task `jolt.deps/a-missing-local-root-is-unresolved`. Measured (fresh HOME, niced): :mvn/version cold 433 ms (one POM and jar over HTTPS), warm 1 ms; :git/sha cold 1433 ms (clone), warm 0 ms; a missing coordinate answered in 440 ms. Not in this task: `add-deps` and `sync-deps` at the REPL, checksums. Wiki: docs/source.md#dependencies."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.fx-test/a-dependency-is-resolved-through-its-owner}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-merge-gate-runs-through-prove"
  :seon.task/plan "Done in part (worktree dry-run, 2026-10-23): the merge gate runs a merge's deferred process tests, only when root asks (`my.root/gate!`), through the `:tests` effect, `seon.fx/run-tests`, whose `seon.fx/test-command` is prove's entry (`bin/prove --tests` of exactly those tests); `seon.fx/test-results` reads its RED lines, and a run that exits non-zero without naming a test says :error for it, never :pass. Also open: the candidate's files are its changed namespaces' only; a changed schema resource is not written over the checkout. Proof: the gate's run names exactly the deferred tests to prove's entry."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.fx-test/the-merge-gate-runs-exactly-the-deferred-tests}}
 {:seon.task/name "deps-at-the-repl"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: the :deps effect (`seon.fx/resolve-deps`) resolves a deps map through Jolt's `jolt.deps/resolve-deps` and answers the source roots or :seon.fx/unresolved; there is no add-lib or sync-deps at the REPL and no checksum (docs/source.md#dependencies; seon.fx/a-dependency-is-fetched-through-its-owner left them out). Correct, with Clojure's names (clojure.repl.deps): add-lib, add-libs and sync-deps ask the :deps effect through `my.fx/call`, load the answered roots in the agent's process and record the coordinate on its branch; a resolved jar or git tree is checked against a sha256 recorded at its first resolve, and a mismatch is the effect's typed error."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.fx-test/add-lib-loads-a-resolved-library seon.fx-test/a-changed-artifact-is-refused-by-its-checksum}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-rate-limited-requeue-reads-what-its-owner-sets"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `seon.fx/owner-proc`'s :done arm re-queues a :seon.model/rate-limited outcome behind three guards on values its own procs always set: `(and limited? (:request m))` though `seon.fx/worker-proc` always sends :request; `(or (::qk r) [...])` though `seon.fx/dispatch` sets ::qk on everything it sends a worker; `(if (vector? qk) (second qk) 0)` though qk is always a vector; plus a :seq bump re-insertion under the old key may not need (check). Merged from an agent's run (573f12f) with the guards the audit graded C+. Correct: read :request and ::qk as given; a message without them is a defect the contract names. Deletes: the three guards (and the :seq bump if it proves unneeded)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.fx-admit-test/a-rate-limited-model-call-waits-for-the-bucket-and-is-answered}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-effect-opens-a-receipt"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: an agent records an effect only when its answer comes (`seon.agent` writes :seon.fx/status :ok|:error with the outcome), so an agent that dies with a :file, :git or :model call in flight leaves no record that it asked, and a resumed branch cannot tell sent from unsent. v1 committed a receipt before the handler ran, settled it once, and never re-sent it on recovery (~/src/seon/src/seon/effect.clj:1-9). Correct: for a non-idempotent kind, the agent writes :seon.fx/status :open with kind, request and the form's ordinal on its branch before `my.fx/call` sends; the answer settles that entity and links the eval; the reconciler marks a dead agent's open receipt :interrupted and never re-sends it. Foundation for `seon.fx/files-and-shell-for-any-work`."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.fx-test/a-dead-agents-open-effect-is-interrupted-not-resent seon.fx-test/an-effect-links-its-eval}}
 {:seon.task/name "files-and-shell-for-any-work"
  :seon.task/plan "Seon does any work its user wants (AGENTS §1; owner 2026-10-22). What exists: the effect kinds in resources/seon/system.edn :fx are :echo :file :source :git :deps :build :model; `seon.fx/write-file` writes any path with no prior-digest check; there is no my.fs, my.edit or my.shell. Correct: :fs (read, glob, stat, write with the prior digest as precondition), :edit (exact-span text and line windows) and :shell (argv only, cwd, an env allowlist, a stdin cap, bounded by bytes not a clock) kinds, with my.fs, my.edit and my.shell as thin wrappers over `my.fx/call`. v1's handler policy becomes config keys (:seon.config.fs/*, :seon.config.shell/env-allowlist; v1 src/seon/fs/jvm.clj:21-127, shell/jvm.clj:73). Each function gets a section in docs/agent.md. Deletes: write-file's unchecked path, folded into the :fs write."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.fx/an-effect-opens-a-receipt"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.fx-test/a-write-needs-the-prior-digest seon.fx-test/shell-answers-exit-and-digested-output seon.fx-test/shell-sees-only-allowed-env}}
 {:seon.task/name "web-fetch-and-search"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: no :web effect, so an agent cannot fetch a page or search (parity.md gap 11). v1: `my.web/fetch` and `search`, http(s) only, no userinfo in the URL, manual redirects with loop detection, a body cap (~/src/seon/src/seon/web/jvm.clj:42,156,297-334); the search key held by root, named by an env-var config key. Correct: :web-fetch and :web-search kinds in system.edn :fx, the search key read only by root's owner (as `seon.fx/model` reads the model key), with my.web as a wrapper over `my.fx/call`; caps as :seon.config.web/* keys."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.fx/an-effect-opens-a-receipt"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.fx-test/redirects-are-bounded seon.fx-test/search-key-stays-in-root}}
 {:seon.task/name "write-back-answers-what-happened"
  :seon.task/plan "Done: `seon.fx/write-file` writes path.fx-PID and renames it over path with rename(2) (`seon.lib/c-rename`, with errno): a failed rename answers :seon.fx/io-error {:path :tmp :errno :message} and removes the tmp; success answers {:value {:bytes n :path path}}, n the path's size on disk after the rename. Replaces an ignored `.renameTo` boolean, which answered success for a rename that failed (a missing directory, a cross-device path, a permission) and left the tmp, so the loop's last step (merged code written back) could record a write that never reached disk. One syscall more; constant, no cost proof."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.fx-test/a-failed-rename-is-an-io-error-and-leaves-no-tmp
                      seon.fx-test/a-write-back-answers-the-bytes-on-disk}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-transient-model-failure-parks"
  :seon.task/plan "Done: a :seon.model/rate-limited outcome (the provider's 429) is not answered to its asker: `seon.fx/owner-proc` records the :effect problem, `seon.fx/settle` halves the bucket's rate (AIMD), and the request the worker performed goes back on the queue under its old key, so `seon.fx/dispatch` sends it again when the bucket refills; the agent's fx! stays parked on its one answer, the call that went through or a non-transient error. This is the delayed insert AGENTS.md section 4 allows for a provider's retry-after. A transport failure (`seon.model/http-post`'s caught exception, no status) is not transient by any provider's statement: it answers the agent and ends the run :error, recorded; re-sending it would be a blind retry. `seon.model/http-post` has no deadline (the reply ends when the server ends it). Per 429: one queue insert, and no model call beyond those the provider refused."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.fx-admit-test/a-rate-limited-model-call-waits-for-the-bucket-and-is-answered}}
 {:seon.task/name "git-is-read-through-fx"
  :seon.task/plan "Done: seon.fx runs git in one place, git-run, read two ways, git! (a failure throws its words) and git-try (git's no, exit 1, is nil; any other exit throws as git! does), and tells a submodule's checkout by its .git through checked-out?; read-source, tree-id, base-commit, git-commit, checkout-at and run-tests use them instead of sh/sh git, and run-tests answers a branch with no commit as a typed error instead of running at an empty sha; my.root/git-refs went for seon.fx/git-refs (git rev-parse --git-common-dir, never the .git file parsed by hand). Law seon.main-test/git-is-read-through-fx, which also sees git run from a shell line. Found by the one-offs survey (the archived paper one-offs-2026-10-23.md (git history); owner, 2026-10-23: src resists growth so agents reuse and improve the core parts rather than making one-offs, DECISIONS). Item 3. What exists: git read outside its owner. my.root/git-refs parses .git, its gitdir: file and commondir by hand; inside seon.fx, tree-id, base-commit and run-tests shell git with sh/sh beside the private git!; bin/tree is the shell's own tree id (seon.check-test/bin-tree-names-the-tree-tree-id-names holds it to fx/tree-id). Correct: seon.fx exposes the git reads others need (the common dir, a revision's commit, a non-throwing read where an absent answer is meaningful), and every Clojure caller goes through them. A law (seon.main-test, the compiler's facts): no src form outside seon.fx names the \"git\" command. Watch: the file watcher starts before effects exist, so it calls the plain function, not an effect."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.main-test/git-is-read-through-fx}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-tree-run-is-one-function"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by the one-offs survey (the archived paper one-offs-2026-10-23.md (git history); owner, 2026-10-23: src resists growth so agents reuse and improve the core parts rather than making one-offs, DECISIONS). Item 5. What exists: seon.fx/build and seon.fx/run-tests repeat one mechanism (the env -u list, bin/worktree --at, a log file, a niced command in that tree, the cleanup), and test-results re-parses prove's stdout with regexes while seon.proofs holds each test's result by digest. Correct: one private in-tree runner both call, and run-tests reads seon.proofs' stored results instead of text. Proof: run-tests and seon.proofs agree on a red fixture. Risk: whether the stored results can be read from the child tree's results directory."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "a-test-digests-the-files-it-read"
  :seon.task/plan "Follow-up to task seon.program/no-test-takes-the-tree (owner, 2026-10-27: \"no tests should include all of src\"; \"I don't care if it's perfect\"). Tests are digested by what they reach, not by the tree, so a file a test reads that its forms do not name (docs/ for the docs tests, config) or the binary it runs changes without rerunning it until the full run (`bin/test --all`). Better, still cheap: record the files each test read in its last run and digest their content (a test with no record digests by reach alone). Built and set aside, on branch `jolt/a-test-digests-what-it-read` (commit 9fccfab234): Jolt already keeps a read sink (`io-file-read-sink`, host/chez/java/io.ss); Jolt branch `jolt/file-reads-are-recorded` (seantempesta/jolt, tip 38ca09f7, on 67e7bf3e) adds `jolt.host/start-file-reads!` and `take-file-reads!` (a process-wide, nesting log, test/chez/file-reads-test.clj); Seon side: `seon.proofs/run-tests!` records each test's reads (relative-reads), `store-verdicts!` writes `<results>/reads/<test>.edn` (reads-file), `seon.fx/inputs` :reads folds them with the tree's blobs (reads-part), `seon.program/graph-index` takes :reads, the shard stores a result at the digest of the files its run read. Needs: a Jolt pin bump (coordinate with the forks session's chain, tip 2ceb9ddb: one bump), the cost proofs a pin bump runs, and the proofs of that branch (seon.fx-test/a-reads-part-..., seon.proofs-test/a-run-records-the-files-the-test-read)."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "the-standing-root-follows-main-not-the-checkouts-head"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-29: `bin/update` merges `jolt/merges` into main (\"Merge jolt/merges: the standing root's merges\"), and the standing root advances `jolt/merges` to the main tree's checked-out HEAD: while the main tree was on the held topic branch jolt/main-notice-commit, that branch's three commits (c0a9fdde89, 46c1d2929f, 10c98b1e7d) reached main at 7dfd1f7b75 with no review, no prove of the merged tree and a +30 MB `e2e calc` regression unexplained. Correct: the standing root's source (seon.fx/read-source, the watcher, :seon.config.source/root) follows main's commit, never the checkout's HEAD; `jolt/merges` holds only the root's own write-back commits; `bin/update` merges only those, and refuses when the main tree is not on main. Until then the rule (memory): never run bin/update with the main tree on a topic branch. Also owed: review the three commits now on main as if they were merging (the +30 MB; the review's fixes on branch jolt/main-notice-commit: the render proc's stale-notice skip, the conn exception note in docs/machine.md)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.source-test/the-standing-root-follows-main-not-the-checkouts-head}}]
